# Documentation layers

**Rule Rev.1** — 2026-09-13 (common)

**Mental model: the spec contains Help** (spec ⊃ Help).
The public specification is the source of truth. Help and the docs site are
**projections** for end users — not a second source of truth, and not a dump
of the whole spec.

| Layer | Audience | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Public spec (`docs/spec.md` or project equivalent) | Public | Current behaviour, invariants, schemas, *what Help should cover* |
| Author notes (`private_spec.md`, gitignored) | Author | Motives, location, discarded ideas. Not Help, not the docs site |
| In-app Help | End users | Short how-to + docs-site pointer + version. No design rationale |
| Docs site | End users / installers | Install, FAQ, troubleshooting — what a public release needs |

CLI `--help` / `--version` are English. They are not language-pack UI copy.

## Agent rules

1. When adding user-facing guidance, ask: **Help**, **docs site**, **public spec**, or **author notes**?
2. Do not put “why we decided…” essays into Help strings.
3. If the spec says “document X for users”, put X on the **docs site / FAQ**, unless it is the short how-to that belongs in Help.
4. If Help and the spec disagree, fix Help to match the spec’s intended user projection, then sync the docs site.

A project may add a Help-budget overlay (what is / is not in Help) next to this file.

